WHEREAS prolonged construction inactivity and incomplete construction projects can have significant impacts on surrounding neighbourhoods, including visual blight, safety concerns, property maintenance issues, and reduced enjoyment of adjacent properties;
WHEREAS residents living adjacent to long-term construction sites may experience these impacts for months or years, despite construction approvals having been granted with the expectation that work would proceed in a timely manner;
WHEREAS construction fencing and site screening play an important role in protecting public safety while also contributing to the appearance and character of residential neighbourhoods, heritage districts, and mainstreets;
THEREFORE BE IT RESOLVED THAT the recommendations contained in ACS2026-OCC-CCS-0064 be amended to include a review of measures available to address prolonged construction inactivity, incomplete construction projects, and situations commonly referred to as "construction by neglect," where properties remain in a partially completed, unsecured, or deteriorated state for extended periods of time;
BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED THAT staff review opportunities to strengthen requirements related to site maintenance, screening, and construction fencing for long-term construction sites, including consideration of enhanced standards for visually appealing fencing and hoarding in urban residential areas, heritage districts, traditional mainstreets, and other sensitive community settings;
BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED THAT staff examine potential enforcement tools and best practices from other municipalities to encourage timely completion of construction projects and reduce prolonged impacts on surrounding neighbourhoods.

WHEREAS David and Linda Darwin have called Carlington home for almost 53 years; and
WHEREAS David and Linda Darwin have significantly contributed to various community, social and religious events in the community for decades, including as Past President and other positions with the Carlington Community Association for David and various church positions at St Elizabeth Parish, Girl Guides, Scouts and St Elizabeth School for both David and Linda; and
WHEREAS, since 2015, the Darwins took it upon themselves to plant, purchase, manicure, water and maintain the Merivale Triangle, a City of Ottawa greenspace bordered by Merivale, Carling and Merivale and in later years provide advice and encouragement to other community volunteers and authored a number of reports to the community association;
WHEREAS in recognition of the Darwins’ contributions within the Carlington community, including at the Merivale Triangle, there is strong support to honour their efforts with a commemorative tree planting; and
WHEREAS the Carlington Community Association and David and Linda Darwin support the proposed tree planting in their name;
THEREFORE BE IT RESOLVED that a commemorative tree be planted in the Merivale Triangle with a small plaque acknowledging David and Linda Darwin for their community service, and that all costs associated with this initiative be covered by the local Councillor for Ward 16;
BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED THAT City staff work with the local Councillor to coordinate the timing of the planting given the current and anticipated integrated infrastructure renewal work in the immediate area.
